1. Scope and ownership

Sergiu Lefter grants this license only for original emu8086-web material for
which he controls the licensing rights ("Covered Material"). It covers that
material in source and built form, including when incorporated into a modified
version. Each copyright holder retains ownership of their work. This is a
source-available license. It is not an OSI-approved open source license.

2. Definitions

"You" means the individual or organization exercising these permissions.
"Authorized Users" are you and members of a defined private group whom you
authorize for a personal, educational, testing or internal purpose, such as
your household, enrolled class or work team. A group open to anyone who signs
up, pays or follows a public invitation is public.

"Private Use" means use by Authorized Users alone for one of those purposes.
"Substantial Functionality" means a core capability of the Covered Material,
such as assembling, running or debugging 8086 programs, even when accessed
through a different interface or as part of a larger product.

"Public Hosting" means operating a publicly accessible copy of the Covered
Material, or providing the general public with network access to Substantial
Functionality implemented by it. This includes websites, online applications,
embedded emulators and APIs, whether free or paid. Requiring a login does
not by itself make a service private. A hosted emulator or API that anyone
can register to use is Public Hosting, even if registration is free.

3. Permissions

Subject to this license, anyone may view, study and download the source.
You have a worldwide, nonexclusive, royalty-free license to run the software
locally and to copy, build, modify and self-host it for Private Use. You may
make backups and provide Authorized Users with the source or built copies
they need for that use. Keep those copies within the group and provide this
license with them. No separate permission or payment is required.

You may run the software on a local computer, including on localhost, and
host it on a personal server, a restricted LAN or a VPN for Authorized
Users. A teacher may host it for an enrolled class. A company may host it
on an internal server for designated employees. An internet-connected server
qualifies for Private Use if access to the software and its functionality
is restricted to Authorized Users and the service is not offered to the public.

4. GitHub and contributions

You may use GitHub's repository features to view and fork the public source
and submit changes to the project by pull request. This license preserves
the rights granted under GitHub's applicable platform terms. Serving a working
public emulator from a fork, including through GitHub Pages, is Public Hosting
under section 5. Section 7 preserves public hosting rights under MIT.

Before submitting a pull request, read and expressly accept the following
contribution terms in the pull request. You retain ownership of your work.
For original material you submit under these terms, you grant Sergiu Lefter
a perpetual, worldwide, nonexclusive, royalty-free, irrevocable license to
use, reproduce, modify, publicly host, distribute, sublicense and relicense
your contribution, including commercially and under different licenses in
future releases. This grant is limited to rights you control or are
authorized to grant on the rights holder's behalf. Identify any third-party
material you submit, its source and its terms.

Earlier contributions remain under the terms on which they were submitted.
Applying this contribution grant to an earlier submission requires the
rights holder's separate express agreement.

